---
title: How to Set Up Job Alerts That Only Send Jobs Worth Applying To
publishedAt: 2026-11-27
type: job-seekers
description: >-
  Set up daily or weekly job alerts by role, location, salary and experience, so
  your inbox gets new matching jobs and skips the ones you've applied to.
---
**Quick answer:** A good job alert is a saved search narrow enough that most results are worth opening. Pick one role, your real locations, a salary floor and your experience range, then choose a daily or weekly email. On GetHired, search on the jobs page, apply your filters and create an alert from that search. Each email lists jobs posted since the last one, leaves out jobs you've applied to, and has a one-click pause link.

Most people turn job alerts off within a few weeks. The usual reason isn't that alerts don't work. It's that one broad alert sends a long list of roles you'd never apply to, so you stop opening it.

## How do you create a job alert on GetHired?

1. Open [Discover jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs) and search the way you normally would.
2. Apply your filters until the results look like a list you'd actually apply from.
3. Create an alert with the bell button in the toolbar, or from the alert banner in the job list.
4. Choose **daily** or **weekly**.

If you aren't signed in, creating an alert takes you to sign-up and brings your search along with you.

You can manage every alert in **Settings → Job alerts**.

## Which filters can a job alert use?

{% table %}
-
- Filter
- How to use it
---
- Job title keywords
- Use the titles employers write, such as "backend engineer" or "data analyst"
---
- Location
- Add every place you'd really work. Common spellings, such as Bangalore and Bengaluru, are treated as the same place
---
- Job type
- Full time, internship and so on
---
- Minimum salary
- A yearly amount in one currency. Salaries are only compared within the same currency
---
- Experience
- A minimum and maximum, to leave out roles far above or below your level
---
- Company
- Follow specific employers you want to work for
---
- Platform
- Limit results to where the job was listed
{% /table %}

## How often should job alerts arrive?

Choose based on how quickly you want to act.

{% table %}
-
- Frequency
- Good for
- When it's sent
---
- Daily
- An active search, narrow titles, roles that fill quickly
- Once a day, in the morning UTC
---
- Weekly
- A passive search, or a few dream companies
- Mondays (UTC)
{% /table %}

An email is sent only when there are new jobs. A quiet day means no email, not an empty one.

## What's in each alert email?

- **Up to 10 jobs** that match the alert.
- **Only new jobs.** Each email covers jobs posted since the alert was last checked, going back 14 days at most, so resuming a paused alert doesn't send you a backlog.
- **No repeats of your applications.** Jobs you've applied to through GetHired are left out.
- **A pause link.** One click pauses that alert without signing in.

## How do you stop job alerts from becoming noise?

### Make one alert per search, not one for everything

"Frontend engineer, remote" and "product designer, Pune" are two searches, so make two alerts. Mixing them means half of every email is irrelevant. You can have up to **5 active alerts** (and 20 saved in total, including paused ones).

### Set a salary floor in the right currency

A salary filter compares amounts only within one currency. If you're searching in INR and USD, create a separate alert for each.

### Use the experience range

A "senior" title means different things at different companies. A years-of-experience range is often a better filter than the word "senior".

### Review after two weeks

Open your last few alert emails and count how many jobs you clicked. If it's almost none, tighten the title or location. If emails rarely arrive, remove one filter, usually salary or company, and see what changes.

## What should you do when an alert finds a good job?

Alerts only help if you act on them:

1. **Save it** if you can't apply now. Saved jobs sit on the Saved tab of your [application tracker](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/job-application-tracker-how-to-keep-track-of-every-application).
2. **Tailor your resume** to the posting. Here's [how to do it without making things up](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-tailor-your-resume-for-each-job-without-making-things-up).
3. **Apply, then record it,** so you know when to follow up.

## Frequently asked questions

### How many job alerts can I have on GetHired?

Up to 5 active alerts at once, and up to 20 saved alerts in total. Pause one to free up an active slot.

### Will I get the same job twice?

Each email covers jobs posted since the alert was last checked, and jobs you've applied to through your account are left out.

### Can I pause a job alert without deleting it?

Yes. Use the pause link in any alert email, or turn the alert off in Settings. You can resume it later with the same filters.

### What's the difference between job alerts and an AI job search agent?

Alerts email you new listings that match fixed filters. An [AI job search agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-use-an-ai-job-search-agent-find-track-and-apply-to-jobs-from-one-chat) takes requests in plain English and lets you refine results in conversation. Many people use both.

### Should I choose daily or weekly job alerts?

Choose daily if you're actively applying and want to see roles soon after they're posted. Choose weekly if you're only keeping an eye on the market.

[Search jobs on GetHired](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs) and turn your best search into an alert.
